From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Artyom Shalkhakov <artyom.shalkhakov@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +0500
Subject: [PATCH] Retain the sender of an action until its caller is done with it

-[NSMenu performActionForItemAtIndex:] sends the item's action and then
posts NSMenuDidSendActionNotification naming the menu. -[NSTextField
textDidEndEditing:] sends the field's action and then asks its window
for the first responder, and may select its text again. Neither retains
the receiver across the action, so an action that releases its sender
-- a form that regenerates all of its widgets when a value changes,
dropping the popup or the field that was just used -- leaves the rest
of the method running on a freed object: objc_retain on the freed
NSMenu inside NSNotificationCenter, or a read of the freed field's
_window.

Cocoa keeps the sender of an action alive for the duration of the event
dispatch, so application code written this way is safe there and dies
only on GNUstep. A popup that commits a choice and makes its owner
rebuild the enclosing form is a reliable trigger. The NSMenu case is
the one reproduced and traced; the NSTextField case is the same shape,
found by reading the code beside it, and is patched the same way.

Retain the receiver for the current autorelease scope before sending
the action, in both places.

One related case is left untouched: -[NSControl mouseDown:] goes on
using its cell after the action returns, and nothing in gnustep-gui
holds the control for it, so a control destroyed by its own action is
still unsafe there.

gnustep-gui's NSMenu, NSMenuItem, NSPopUpButton, NSTextField and
NSControl suites pass with the change.

A reproduction program, nsmenu-action-releases-sender.m, accompanies
this patch: on an unpatched library valgrind reports the invalid read
in -performActionForItemAtIndex: and MALLOC_PERTURB_ makes it a
segfault; with the change it survives.
---
Keep the sender of an action alive until the action's caller is done with it

-[NSMenu performActionForItemAtIndex:] sends the item's action and then
posts NSMenuDidSendActionNotification naming the menu. -[NSTextField
textDidEndEditing:] sends the field's action and then asks its window for
the first responder, and may select its text again. Neither retains the
receiver across the action, so an action that releases its sender -- a
form that regenerates all of its widgets when a value changes, dropping
the popup or the field that was just used -- leaves the rest of the
method running on a freed object: objc_retain on the freed NSMenu inside
NSNotificationCenter, or a read of the freed field's _window.

Cocoa keeps the sender of an action alive for the duration of the event
(it is autoreleased by the dispatch), so application code written this
way is safe there and dies only on GNUstep. XFormsViewer's select1 popup
was a reliable trigger: choose an item, the form rebuilds, segfault. The
NSMenu case is the one reproduced and traced; the NSTextField case is the
same shape found by reading the code beside it, and is patched the same
way.

Retain the receiver for the current autorelease scope before sending the
action, in both places.

A standalone reproduction is in nsmenu-action-releases-sender.m: on an
unpatched gnustep-gui, valgrind reports the invalid read in
performActionForItemAtIndex: and MALLOC_PERTURB_ makes it a segfault.
With this patch it survives, and gnustep-gui's NSMenu, NSMenuItem,
NSPopUpButton, NSTextField and NSControl suites pass.

diff --git a/Source/NSMenu.m b/Source/NSMenu.m
index d0a594934..3672ed665 100644
--- a/Source/NSMenu.m
+++ b/Source/NSMenu.m
@@ -1190,6 +1190,13 @@ static BOOL menuBarVisible = YES;
   if (![item isEnabled])
     return;
 
+  /* The action may release its sender -- a form that regenerates its
+   * widgets when a value changes drops the popup, and the popup owns this
+   * menu -- and the notification below still names the receiver.  Keep it
+   * for the current autorelease scope, as Cocoa does for any sender.
+   */
+  IF_NO_ARC(AUTORELEASE(RETAIN(self));)
+
   // Send the actual action and the stipulated notifications.
   d = [NSDictionary dictionaryWithObject: item forKey: @"MenuItem"];
   [nc postNotificationName: NSMenuWillSendActionNotification
diff --git a/Source/NSTextField.m b/Source/NSTextField.m
index 2ebd2772b..0adbd13b5 100644
--- a/Source/NSTextField.m
+++ b/Source/NSTextField.m
@@ -679,6 +679,14 @@ static Class textFieldCellClass;
   id textMovement;
   int movement;
 
+  /* The action sent below may release its sender -- a form that
+   * regenerates its widgets when a value is committed drops this field --
+   * and the code after it still asks _window for the first responder and
+   * may select the text again.  Keep the receiver for the current
+   * autorelease scope, as Cocoa does for any sender.
+   */
+  IF_NO_ARC(AUTORELEASE(RETAIN(self));)
+
   [super textDidEndEditing: aNotification];
 
   textMovement = [[aNotification userInfo] objectForKey: @"NSTextMovement"];
